3. NON-NEGOTIABLE DEVELOPMENT RULES

Follow these rules throughout development.

1. The application must be a real Android application, not a website wrapped inside a WebView.
2. Use native Android UI for the core editing interface.
3. Use C++ for performance-critical components where it provides a meaningful benefit.
4. Use hardware acceleration when available and appropriate.
5. Preserve project editability after closing and reopening the application.
6. Editing operations must be nondestructive wherever practical.
7. All timeline operations must support undo and redo.
8. All media processing must report genuine progress and errors.
9. All export operations must produce verifiable output files.
10. Every user-facing control must either work or be explicitly disabled with an understandable explanation.
11. Never simulate an AI result when the configured provider fails.
12. Never hardcode API keys.
13. Never upload user media to an AI provider without clear user consent.
14. Never silently overwrite project files or exported videos.
15. Never silently reduce export quality without informing the user.
16. Handle missing media, insufficient storage, unsupported codecs, interrupted rendering, and application restarts.
17. Use real tests instead of claiming that a feature works merely because the code compiles.
18. Keep all dependencies compatible with the actual Android build environment.
19. Verify native library support for ARM64 and other architectures that are explicitly targeted.
20. Do not claim that a feature is complete until its implementation, integration, and tests support that claim.

Do not try to implement the entire application in one enormous source file.

Create a maintainable project structure with clear modules, interfaces, documentation, and automated tests.

