36. TECHNICAL IMPLEMENTATION STRATEGY

Before selecting libraries, evaluate their current Android compatibility, maintenance status, licensing, ABI support, and performance.

Potential technologies to evaluate include:

- Kotlin
- Jetpack Compose
- Android View-based components where more suitable
- Android MediaCodec
- MediaExtractor
- MediaMuxer
- Android NDK
- CMake
- OpenGL ES
- Vulkan where appropriate
- FFmpeg
- Oboe or another suitable low-latency audio framework
- SQLite or Room for metadata
- Android Keystore
- Coroutines and Flow
- A compatible 3D rendering engine

Do not blindly include every technology listed.

Select a coherent set of dependencies based on actual implementation needs.

36.1 Media Engine Strategy

Build a media abstraction layer.

Separate:

- Demuxing
- Decoding
- Frame scheduling
- Video processing
- Audio processing
- Compositing
- Encoding
- Muxing
- Metadata inspection

Use Android-native media APIs when they provide the necessary functionality and compatibility.

Evaluate FFmpeg when its filtering, demuxing, encoding, or muxing capabilities are required.

Maintain a clear license inventory.

If distributing FFmpeg, codecs, or other native libraries, verify the applicable licenses and redistribution obligations.

Do not bundle incompatible or prohibited codecs.

36.2 GPU Strategy

Use GPU acceleration for operations that benefit from it.

Evaluate:

- GPU transforms
- Texture compositing
- Color correction
- LUT processing
- Blur
- Glow
- Mask rendering
- Motion graphics
- 3D rendering

Use appropriate CPU fallbacks when necessary.

Avoid unnecessary texture copies and CPU-GPU synchronization.

Handle GPU resource loss and context recreation.

36.3 Memory Strategy

Never decode an entire long video into RAM.

Use:

- Streaming decoding
- Bounded caches
- Lazy thumbnail generation
- Memory-aware image loading
- Proxy workflows
- Native buffer reuse
- Explicit resource lifetimes
- Background task cancellation

Prevent memory leaks across JNI boundaries.

Use appropriate ownership rules for native objects.

36.4 Build Strategy

Maintain:

- A reproducible Gradle build
- Compatible dependency versions
- Native build configuration
- ABI configuration
- Debug and release variants
- Signing configuration
- ProGuard/R8 rules where needed
- CI-compatible tests
- Dependency and license documentation

Never commit private signing keys or API credentials.

